WARNING:  [JEP 411] migration advisory: Java version 24 and later cannot run PL/Java 1.6.10 with policy enforcement
DETAIL:  This PL/Java version enforces security policy using important Java features that upstream Java has disabled as of Java 24, as described in JEP 486. In Java 18 through 23, enforcement is still available, but requires "-Djava.security.manager=allow" in "pljava.vmoptions".
HINT:  For migration planning, this version of PL/Java can still enforce policy in Java versions up to and including 23, and Java 17 and 21 are positioned as long-term support releases. Java 24 and later can be used, if wanted, WITH ABSOLUTELY NO EXPECTATIONS OF SECURITY POLICY ENFORCEMENT, by adding "-Djava.security.manager=disallow" in "pljava.vmoptions". This mode should be considered only if all Java code to be used is considered well vetted and trusted. For details on how PL/Java will adapt, please bookmark https://github.com/tada/pljava/wiki/JEP-411
-- PL/Java 1.6 loads classes from the sqlj jar repository, which WarehousePG
-- replicates to every segment. Install the examples jar (no deploy: the test
-- creates its own functions) and give the javatest schema a classpath to it.
SELECT sqlj.install_jar('file:/usr/local/whpg/Versions/2.1.0/share/postgresql/pljava/pljava-examples-1.6.10.jar', 'examples', false);
 install_jar 
-------------
 
(1 row)

CREATE SCHEMA javatest AUTHORIZATION pljava_test;
SELECT sqlj.set_classpath('javatest', 'examples');
 set_classpath 
---------------
 
(1 row)

-- 1.6 revokes USAGE on the trusted language from PUBLIC; the tests run as
-- pljava_test, so grant it back explicitly.
GRANT USAGE ON LANGUAGE java TO pljava_test;
-- pljava.control says schema = sqlj, so CREATE EXTENSION pre-creates the schema
-- and PL/Java's own groundwork leaves its permissions untouched: public has no
-- USAGE on sqlj, which the class loader needs when a non-superuser calls in.
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA sqlj TO public;
alter database pljava_test owner to pljava_test;
\c pljava_test pljava_test
